You’re right — I over-focused on the current implementation path and accidentally framed the goal as “make the existing six-page-style output work in React.” That is not the product direction.

The legacy six-page chart should remain a **reference/comparison artifact**. The real design target is an honest upgrade over it, starting from `compv3`.

# Design brief: Compact-v4 direction

## 1. Honest verdict on the current mock

### What the mock gets right

`design/panchavati-compv3.html` is a strong starting point because it already has:

- a chart-like document grammar instead of a dashboard;
- low-noise navy/white/slate palette;
- compact row-based staff display;
- a useful command header;
- a reasonable page-1 hierarchy;
- a good six-chowki matrix direction;
- separate leave, manpower, officer and scan information;
- explicit visual distinctions for `रिक्त`, `बंद` and `बिमा`;
- no dependency on the old six-page visual structure.

### What is not yet good enough

The mock was designed with representative/sample data, so it looks more efficient than it is under the full 10/09 data volume.

Main issues:

1. **Page 1 is overloaded**
   - Sr-PI;
   - officer boxes;
   - day deployment;
   - night deployment;
   - office;
   - court/special desk;
   - several long role groups.

   It becomes vertically excessive once real source rows are inserted.

2. **The officer boxes consume too much vertical space**
   - They are useful as a concept.
   - Their current box treatment creates repeated borders and empty padding.
   - They should become a compact command strip or a tight officer matrix, not dashboard-like cards.

3. **Every block feels boxed**
   - The outer table border;
   - block border;
   - sub-table border;
   - row border;
   - chip border.

   That produces the “double border / clutter / AI-generated admin UI” feeling you dislike.

4. **Page 3 has uneven density**
   - One long leave list can force neighbouring columns to leave large empty areas.
   - The current card/grid approach wastes space when categories have very different lengths.

5. **The mock has a dangerous page-fit illusion**
   - Sample data fits.
   - Real 10/09 data does not fit at the same comfortable scale.
   - We must optimize against maximum realistic source density, not against the sample screenshot.

6. **Page count should not be the optimization target**
   - Four tiny unreadable pages are worse than six well-packed pages.
   - The goal is maximum usable information per area without making Marathi difficult to read.

## 2. Core visual principle

The new chart should be:

> **A clean command document composed of horizontal bands and disciplined columns — not a collection of cards.**

### Remove from the chart itself

- rounded cards;
- shadows;
- decorative pills;
- unnecessary nested borders;
- large empty card padding;
- dashboard KPI tiles;
- oversized section headers;
- repeated page headers taking excessive height;
- labels repeated in every staff row;
- full names repeated everywhere.

### Keep

- one page frame;
- thin section rules;
- restrained table gridlines;
- clear section bands;
- compact source-faithful grouping;
- text-based state labels;
- small color accents;
- full data available through hover/detail editing.

---

# 3. Proposed chart grammar

## Outer page

Each printed sheet should have:

1. a compact 1-line identity header;
2. a thin date/status line;
3. two to four horizontal content bands;
4. no card shadow;
5. one consistent page border or no border, not several nested frames.

### Header target

```text
पंचवटी पोलीस ठाणे, नाशिक शहर       दैनिक कर्तव्य तक्ता       दिनांक १०/०९/२०२६ · गुरुवार
```

- 10–12px print title;
- 8–9px metadata;
- lifecycle shown as a small text label, not a large pill;
- header height reduced substantially;
- repeated on each print sheet only because it is operationally useful.

## Section band

Use a narrow section band:

```text
01  दिवसपाळी — ०९:०० ते २१:००                         ८२ कर्मचारी
```

- number optional and very small;
- title left aligned;
- time right aligned;
- one 1px rule below;
- no rounded container;
- count is informational and subtle.

## Row grammar

Every staff row should have a predictable form:

```text
role / duty label        staff display name          time / state
```

Example:

```text
रायटर                    पोहवा ६९२ गुंबाडे             —
                         पोशि ५६९ पवार
बिमा                     पोशि १२४४ महाले              बिमा
                         पोशि २१५७ गुंजाळ
```

Rules:

- role labels use `rowspan`;
- role labels are not repeated for every person;
- staff display name uses compact source form;
- full master name remains available in editor/detail view;
- time is a small right-side value;
- role, name and state remain separate fields;
- no inline explanatory prose unless it is source content;
- no value is truncated;
- two-line wrap is allowed;
- avoid one-word orphan lines.

## Staff display

For dense chart display:

```text
rank + buckle + surname
```

Examples:

```text
पोशि ५४२ आहेर
पोहवा ६९२ गुंबाडे
पोनि सुनिल पवार
```

In the editor/detail panel:

```text
Full name: पोहवा ६९२ अनिल बाळु गुंबाडे
Rank: HC
Buckle: 692
Source/status/history...
```

This gives density without losing identity.

## State display

Use text first, color second.

### `रिक्त`

```text
रिक्त
```

- dashed underline or dashed empty-seat rule;
- amber text;
- no filled pill.

### `बंद`

```text
बंद · चार्ज → मालेगाव स्टँड
```

- one compact state row;
- muted hatch or pale yellow background;
- `बंद` never confused with a vacant assignment.

### `बिमा`

```text
पोशि १२४४ महाले   बिमा
```

- small red text marker;
- no large red pill;
- remains assignment-level only.

---

# 4. Proposed page/section strategy

This is **not a recreation of the old six-page format**. It is a new logical grouping that may still print to five or six adaptive sheets when the real data requires it.

## Sheet 1 — Command + daytime deployment

### Top band: command strip

Replace large officer cards with a compact command strip:

```text
वरिष्ठ पोलीस निरीक्षक: गजेंद्र पाटील
रायटर: ...
चालक दिवस: ...
चालक रात्र: ...
```

Then a compact officer matrix:

```text
अधिकारी        शाखा/भूमिका       आजची नेमणूक
पोनि ...       क्राईम             उपस्थित
सपोनि ...      तपास              ०९–१४
...
```

This preserves the officer information but avoids four large boxes.

### Main body: three lanes

```text
दिवसपाळी मुख्य ठिकाणे | मोबाईल / DB / लॉकअप | कार्यालयीन / विशेष
```

Why three lanes:

- the existing four equal columns make day/night columns too narrow;
- office/special roles have different density from station deployment;
- the chart should not force unrelated rows into equal-height cards.

Use a continuous table within each lane, not a card.

### Daytime content

Include:

- station leadership;
- station amaldar;
- hazari;
- CCTNS;
- wireless;
- Peter/CR/Delta/DB;
- lockup;
- special desk/office roles that actually belong to daytime.

Do not place all chowki people here if they are shown in the dedicated chowki sheet. Avoid duplicate rendering.

## Sheet 2 — Night deployment + instructions

Night data is structurally different from day data.

Do not force night instructions into fake staff rows.

Use:

```text
रात्रपाळी कर्मचारी lanes        रात्रपाळी सूचना
```

Left side:

- checking officer;
- PSO;
- hazari;
- CCTNS;
- wireless;
- mobile teams;
- DB;
- lockup;
- night special posts.

Right side:

- Good Morning Squad;
- Ramkund patrol;
- statue visits;
- chowki rounds;
- DB instructions;
- app scanning;
- establishment closure instructions.

Instructions should be compact numbered text blocks, not huge bordered cards.

## Sheet 3 — Chowki command matrix

This is the strongest part of the current mock and should remain its own focused sheet.

Use a 3×2 grid:

```text
काटया मारूती | पंचवटी कारंजा | मालेगाव स्टँड
सरदार        | फुलेनगर        | मेरी
```

Each chowki block:

```text
चौकी नाव
प्रभारी / वेळ
दिवसपाळी              रात्रपाळी
staff rows             staff rows
बिमा rows              बिमा rows
मार्शल line            बंद / चार्ज line
```

### Important improvement

Do not put thick borders around each chowki.

Instead:

- use a thin vertical rule between columns;
- use a slightly darker heading underline;
- use a very light background only for the heading row;
- use whitespace between groups;
- use one shared outer grid.

A chowki block should feel like a newspaper column, not a dashboard card.

### Marshal handling

Keep the current data model:

- marshal remains a separate operational post;
- display position may fold into its paired chowki;
- its own closure/charge state remains explicit;
- no BIMA inference.

## Sheet 4 — Leave, other duties, events and notices

This should be a flowing multi-column register, not four independent cards.

Suggested structure:

```text
साप्ताहिक सुट्टी | रजा / सिक | तपास / प्रशिक्षण / प्रतिनियुक्ती | इतर सूचना
```

Use:

- category heading once;
- people beneath it;
- date/time annotations in a narrow secondary column;
- no blank card height matching;
- columns should flow downward independently;
- if one category is longer, it continues naturally beneath the next compatible section rather than leaving empty white space.

For notices:

- use a full-width notice strip below the lists;
- numbered lines;
- no card per notice;
- group by day/night/source scope;
- keep source wording.

For 11/09:

- event/bandobast should appear as an optional annex band or additional adaptive sheet;
- not force Ganpati fields into the ordinary daily chart.

## Sheet 5 — Manpower + change ledger

### Manpower matrix

The matrix should remain table-like because it is genuinely tabular.

Improvements:

- compact two-line column headers;
- freeze/shorten only display labels, never data;
- use hairline grid;
- avoid a large surrounding card;
- show source-entered values exactly;
- clearly mark derived values separately;
- do not “repair” contradictory ME/03 values.

### Change ledger

Put it directly below the matrix with only the required columns:

```text
क्रमांक | बदल / कारण | नोंद
```

Do not leave a giant empty table area if there are no entries. Show:

```text
अचानक बदल नोंद: आज कोणतीही नोंद नाही.
```

and preserve an editor action to add one.

## Sheet 6 — Officer summary + scan schedule + sign-off

### Officer summary

Use a two-column table:

```text
क्र. | अधिकारी | कर्तव्य
```

The current 15-row summary is inherently compact and should not be turned into individual officer boxes.

### Scan schedule

The current hourly scan list should become a time grid:

```text
वेळ       चौकी             कर्मचारी
०९–१०     मेरी             ...
१०–११     मालेगाव         ...
...
```

For screen:

- allow a compact horizontal time grid if wide enough;
- for print, use two side-by-side 6-row tables rather than one tall 12-row table.

Example:

```text
०९–१४                              १५–२१
०९–१० ...                          १५–१६ ...
...
१४–१५ ...                          २०–२१ ...
```

This preserves all 12 rows while reducing vertical waste.

### Sign-off

Keep sign-off as a simple bottom rule and three aligned fields. No card.

---

# 5. Adaptive pagination strategy

Do not hardcode “one logical section equals one physical page.”

Use:

- logical sections in `ChartDocument`;
- content-aware print flow;
- continuation only when necessary;
- repeated compact section headers when a section continues;
- no orphan continuation pages.

### Page-fit rules

For every representative real chart:

- no page-level horizontal overflow;
- no content truncation;
- no row loss;
- no font below the agreed minimum;
- no orphaned section title at page bottom;
- no section forced into a tiny unreadable area;
- no huge empty region when following content can flow into it.

The target is not “fewest pages.” The target is:

> **Fewest readable sheets that preserve every source fact.**

The old six-page packet may remain six or seven sheets in some cases. That is acceptable if the new grammar is visibly cleaner, more compact and easier to scan.

---

# 6. CRUD interaction design

The chart should remain visually clean in preview mode. Editing chrome appears only in edit mode.

## Assignment edit

Click a staff row → open a right-side inspector/flyout.

Inspector:

```text
कर्तव्य: काटया मारूती
पाळी: दिवसपाळी
सध्याची नेमणूक: पोशि ५४२ आहेर

[कर्मचारी बदला]
[रिक्त करा]
[बिमा लागू करा]
[वेळ संपादित करा]
[नोंद पहा]
```

Staff picker:

- search by name/buckle/rank;
- current person pinned at top;
- list uses compact rank + buckle + surname;
- availability warning is informational;
- no automatic replacement;
- user explicitly selects.

## BIMA

Only in the assignment inspector:

```text
बिमा: [ ] फक्त या नेमणुकीसाठी
```

Never infer it from duty title, location or notes.

## Location state

Click the chowki heading or state line:

```text
चौकी: पंचवटी कारंजा
पाळी: दिवसपाळी

स्थिती:
( ) कार्यरत
( ) बंद

चार्ज:
[ destination ]
```

Do not expose closure as a person-row status.

## Add row

Use contextual `+` only at the end of a logical group:

```text
+ कर्मचारी
+ ड्युटी
+ सूचना
```

The `+` should not appear on every row.

## Add chowki/officer block

Keep the existing real persistence behavior, but visually present it as:

- a new column/group in the chart;
- no fake empty card;
- one real vacant slot;
- explicit label;
- no invented person or timing.

## Label editing

Keep the existing per-chart label functionality, but visually:

- pencil appears only on hover/focus in edit mode;
- no layout reflow;
- popover offers:
  - `फक्त हे`;
  - `सर्वत्र`;
- finalized chart remains immutable.

## Names and free text conversion

Future guided conversion:

```text
English input
↓
Marathi suggestion
↓
user reviews/edits
↓
explicit Save
```

This must apply only to approved fields and never silently overwrite names or operational text.

---

# 7. Design verification before implementation

We should not implement blindly. First create a design fixture matrix using real 10/09 data:

### Compare three versions

1. Legacy/reference six-page output.
2. Current compv3 mock with real data.
3. Proposed compact-v4 layout.

Measure:

- physical sheet count;
- printable height used per sheet;
- unused whitespace;
- nested border count;
- repeated labels;
- wrapped rows;
- longest row height;
- page-level overflow;
- source rows rendered;
- readability at normal zoom;
- grayscale distinguishability;
- edit-mode layout stability.

### Minimum acceptance targets

- 212/212 assignment rows represented;
- no page-level horizontal overflow;
- no Marathi truncation;
- no nested card borders in chart content;
- no section title stranded alone at a page bottom;
- no large avoidable blank gaps;
- no print text smaller than the agreed readability floor;
- BIMA/रिक्त/बंद distinguishable without color alone;
- screen preview and print use the same document semantics;
- edit hover does not move rows or alter geometry.

---

# 8. My honest recommendation

Do **not** migrate to React or redesign the navbar yet.

First, build a **static Compact-v4 design prototype** from the existing 10/09 data using the current renderer data shape, then compare it visually against:

- the compv3 mock;
- the current root screenshot;
- the original 10/09 visual pages.

The key redesign is:

1. keep the mock’s logical grouping;
2. remove card treatment;
3. convert officer boxes into a compact command strip;
4. give day, night and chowki content dedicated dense lanes;
5. make leave/notice sections flow independently;
6. convert scan schedule to two compact time grids;
7. use adaptive content-driven pagination;
8. test the layout against full real data before React authoring work.

That will answer the most important question first:

> Is Compact-v4 genuinely more efficient and readable than the original six-page chart, or merely a prettier mock?

I will not implement the React slice until this design direction is approved.You’re right. I over-focused on “making the current implementation work” instead of challenging whether the current design is actually a better chart.

The correct priority is:

> **Design and prove a genuinely better compact chart first. Then implement it.**

The old six-page Panchavati chart remains a **read-only reference/comparison artifact**. The following can appear in the site as disabled `Coming Soon`, `WIP` or `Planned` surfaces, but they are not omitted from the product vision:

- 11/09 event annex;
- legacy six-page viewer;
- OCR import;
- historical search;
- real authentication;
- master-data CRUD;
- leave/manpower/notice/event authoring;
- multi-station support;
- WhatsApp/Telegram/cloud integrations;
- full template builder.

# Compact-v4 design brief

## 1. Honest assessment of the current mock

### The mock is directionally correct

`design/panchavati-compv3.html` is a good starting point because it:

- looks like a chart, not a dashboard;
- has useful compact grouping;
- avoids the old six-page layout;
- keeps day/night/chowki/leave/manpower/scan information conceptually separate;
- has a calmer visual language;
- uses a reasonable dense table grammar;
- keeps `रिक्त`, `बंद` and `बिमा` visually distinct.

### But it is not yet proven to be more efficient

The screenshot:

```text
Current_Screenshots_before_astra\Screenshot_panchavati-compv3_html.png
```

uses sample-sized data. It therefore gives a false impression of how well the layout handles a real day.

The screenshot of the current data-backed implementation:

```text
Current_Screenshots_before_astra\Screenshot_Current_root_127.0.0.1_3100_charts_2026-09-10.png
```

shows the opposite problem:

- too many cards;
- excessive repeated borders;
- large vertical expansion;
- too much dashboard-like framing;
- weak grouping of dense real data;
- page sections that do not use available horizontal space well.

So the honest verdict is:

> **Compv3 is a good information-architecture start, but not a finished high-density chart design.**

We should preserve its grouping ideas while redesigning its geometry and visual grammar.

---

# 2. Core visual strategy

The new chart should feel like:

> **A compact operational command document made from bands, lanes and disciplined tables — not a dashboard made from cards.**

## Remove from the chart

- rounded cards;
- shadows;
- nested boxes;
- double borders;
- large empty padding;
- decorative KPI tiles;
- oversized colored pills;
- repeated labels;
- full names repeated in every row;
- unnecessary icons;
- excessive explanatory text;
- page-level whitespace created only to make cards equal height.

## Keep

- one outer page frame;
- thin section rules;
- small section headers;
- readable dense rows;
- subtle color semantics;
- compact source-faithful groupings;
- explicit operational states;
- full data available in the editor/detail panel.

## Border rule

Inside the printed chart:

- one outer page rule;
- one section divider;
- hairline internal table rules only where they improve scanning;
- no independently bordered card for every office, chowki or officer.

A chowki should look like a newspaper/table column, not a UI card.

---

# 3. Typography and density

The chart needs a deliberately declared scale.

## Print target

Approximate starting range:

- page title: 12–14px;
- section heading: 9.5–11px;
- staff row: 8.8–10px;
- secondary annotation: 8–8.8px;
- table header: 8.5–9.5px.

Do not keep shrinking indefinitely. The chart must remain readable when printed and photographed.

## Name strategy

In the chart:

```text
rank + buckle + surname
```

Examples:

```text
पोशि ५४२ आहेर
पोहवा ६९२ गुंबाडे
पोनि सुनिल पवार
```

In the editor/detail panel:

```text
पूर्ण नाव: पोहवा ६९२ अनिल बाळु गुंबाडे
हुद्दा: HC
बकल: ६९२
```

This preserves identity without wasting chart width.

## Row rules

- Group labels use `rowspan`.
- A label appears once per logical group.
- Staff names continue below it without repeating the label.
- Times use a narrow right-aligned column.
- Roles such as `रायटर`, `चालक`, `बिमा` remain separate fields.
- Long names may wrap to two lines.
- Nothing is truncated.
- No isolated one-word line should be produced accidentally.

---

# 4. Page and section strategy

This is not a recreation of the old six-page chart. It is a new logical arrangement that may still require five or six adaptive print sheets for a large real day.

The goal is not the smallest page count.

> **The goal is the fewest readable sheets that preserve every fact.**

## Sheet 1 — Command strip and daytime core

### Replace the large officer boxes

The current four officer boxes consume too much height and create repeated borders.

Use a compact command strip:

```text
वरिष्ठ पोलीस निरीक्षक: गजेंद्र पाटील
रायटर: ...
चालक दिवस: ...
चालक रात्र: ...
```

Below that, use a compact officer table only when required:

```text
अधिकारी             भूमिका/शाखा        आजची स्थिती
पोनि ...             क्राईम              उपस्थित
सपोनि ...            तपास               ०९–१४
सपोनि ...            प्रशासन            उपस्थित
```

No four large cards.

### Main daytime lanes

Use three unequal horizontal lanes:

```text
दिवसपाळी मुख्य ठिकाणे | मोबाईल / DB / लॉकअप | कार्यालयीन / विशेष
```

They should not be equal width if their content density differs.

Likely widths:

- main station deployment: 42%;
- mobile/DB/lockup: 28%;
- office/special: 30%.

The important improvement is not just shrinking text. It is allocating width according to content.

### Avoid duplicate chowki data

If chowki staff appears in the dedicated chowki sheet, do not repeat all chowki personnel on Sheet 1.

Sheet 1 should focus on:

- station leadership;
- station amaldar;
- hazari;
- CCTNS;
- wireless;
- mobile units;
- DB;
- lockup;
- office/special posts.

## Sheet 2 — Night core and instructions

Night content is not just a second staff list. It contains directive prose.

Use two broad lanes:

```text
रात्रपाळी कर्मचारी              रात्रपाळी सूचना
```

### Left lane

- checking officer;
- night PSO;
- hazari;
- CCTNS;
- wireless;
- mobile;
- DB;
- lockup;
- night special posts.

### Right lane

- Good Morning Squad;
- Ramkund patrol;
- statue visits;
- chowki rounds;
- DB instructions;
- app scanning;
- establishment closure instructions.

Instructions should be:

```text
१. गुडमॉर्निंग स्कॉड...
२. रामकुंड परिसरात...
३. सर्व बीट मार्शल...
```

Do not render them as fake assignment rows or tall cards.

## Sheet 3 — Chowki command matrix

This is the best part of the current mock and should remain focused.

Use a 3×2 arrangement:

```text
काटया मारूती       पंचवटी कारंजा       मालेगाव स्टँड
सरदार              फुलेनगर             मेरी
```

Each block should contain:

```text
चौकी नाव
प्रभारी + वेळ
दिवसपाळी | रात्रपाळी
staff rows
बिमा rows
मार्शल/state line
```

### Visual treatment

Remove the heavy individual card look.

Use:

- one shared outer matrix;
- thin internal separators;
- lightly tinted heading row;
- no shadows;
- no rounded corners;
- no full border around every sub-column;
- whitespace between groups instead of extra boxes.

### Marshal handling

Keep the current semantic model:

- marshal is a separate operational post;
- its display may be folded into the paired chowki;
- its `बंद` and `चार्ज` state remains explicit;
- BIMA is never inferred.

### Example

```text
काटया मारूती चौकी
प्रभारी: सपोनि हेमंत मोहिते · ०९–१४

दिवसपाळी                         रात्रपाळी
पोशि ५४२ आहेर                    पोशि ...
पोशि १९९८ वाकचैरे  बिमा           पोशि ...

मार्शल: बंद · चार्ज → मालेगाव
```

The state line should be one compact line that wraps only the destination if necessary.

## Sheet 4 — Leave, other duties, notices

Do not use equal-height cards.

Use an independently flowing column layout:

```text
साप्ताहिक सुट्टी | रजा / सिक | तपास / प्रशिक्षण | इतर ड्युटी
```

Each column flows naturally according to its own row count.

### Row form

```text
पोशि ५४२ आहेर                 ०८ ते १०
पोनि ...                       २८/०८ ते ११/०९
```

- name on the left;
- date/time annotation on the right;
- category header once;
- no repeated category pill on every row.

### Notices

Place notices in a full-width lower band:

```text
सूचना व निर्देश
१. ...
२. ...
३. ...
```

Do not create one card per notice.

### 11/09 event

The Ganpati event should become:

- a clearly labelled event annex;
- an additional adaptive sheet when present;
- not a forced section inside the ordinary daily chart.

## Sheet 5 — Manpower and change ledger

### Manpower matrix

Keep it as a real matrix, not a card.

Improvements:

- compact two-line headers;
- hairline grid;
- values not bolded unnecessarily;
- clear distinction between source-entered and derived values;
- no automatic reconciliation of `ME`, `03` or contradictory totals;
- preserve `..`, blanks and source artifacts exactly where required.

### Change ledger

When empty, do not print a huge empty table.

Use:

```text
अचानक ड्युटी बदल — आज कोणतीही नोंद नाही.
```

When populated, expand into:

```text
क्रमांक | बदल / कारण | नोंद
```

## Sheet 6 — Officer summary, scans and sign-off

### Officer summary

The 15-row officer summary should be one compact table:

```text
क्र. | अधिकारी | कर्तव्य
```

Do not turn officers into cards here. The command strip already gives the high-level view.

### Scan schedule

Use two side-by-side six-row tables:

```text
०९–१५                              १५–२१
वेळ | चौकी | कर्मचारी              वेळ | चौकी | कर्मचारी
०९–१० ...                          १५–१६ ...
१०–११ ...                          १६–१७ ...
...
१४–१५ ...                          २०–२१ ...
```

This uses horizontal space instead of producing one tall 12-row block.

### Sign-off

Use simple aligned footer fields:

```text
तयार करणारे        तपासणी अधिकारी        मान्यता
```

No footer card.

---

# 5. Adaptive pagination

The renderer should not assume:

```text
one logical section = one physical page
```

Instead:

- sections are logical;
- physical sheets are content-driven;
- a long section may continue;
- continuation gets a compact repeated heading;
- no orphaned heading;
- no blank page created just because a section has a fixed card height.

For example:

- normal day may render 5 sheets;
- a dense day may render 6;
- 11/09 with event annex may render 7;
- a sparse day should not have huge empty regions just to preserve fixed boxes.

This is more honest than promising that every chart always fits the sample mock's page count.

---

# 6. CRUD interaction design

The chart remains clean in Preview mode. Edit mode adds affordances without changing geometry.

## Assignment editing

Click a staff row → open a right-side inspector.

```text
कर्तव्य: काटया मारूती
पाळी: दिवसपाळी
सध्याची नेमणूक: पोशि ५४२ आहेर

[कर्मचारी बदला]
[रिक्त करा]
[बिमा लागू करा]
[वेळ संपादित करा]
[नोंद पहा]
```

The picker:

- searches Marathi name;
- searches English name;
- searches buckle;
- searches rank;
- shows current person first;
- shows availability as a warning only;
- never auto-replaces anyone.

## BIMA

Only an explicit assignment field:

```text
बिमा: [ ] फक्त या नेमणुकीसाठी
```

Never infer it from:

- location;
- role;
- text;
- marshal status;
- notes.

## Chowki state

Click the chowki header or state line:

```text
चौकी: पंचवटी कारंजा
पाळी: दिवसपाळी

स्थिती:
( ) कार्यरत
( ) बंद

चार्ज:
[ destination ]
```

`बंद` is a location-state editor, not a staff-row status.

## Adding rows

Contextual actions should appear only at meaningful group boundaries:

```text
+ कर्मचारी
+ ड्युटी
+ सूचना
+ घटना
```

Do not put `+` on every row.

## Label edits

Keep the existing real per-chart label behavior:

- `फक्त हे`;
- `सर्वत्र`;
- no geometry shift;
- no editing of people or source values through the label editor;
- finalized charts remain immutable.

---

# 7. How we prove this is an improvement

Before React migration, build a data-backed Compact-v4 prototype using the actual 10/09 `ChartDocument`.

Compare:

1. original six-page reference;
2. current compv3 mock with real data;
3. proposed Compact-v4.

Measure:

- sheet count;
- printable height used;
- avoidable whitespace;
- nested-border count;
- repeated labels;
- wrapped rows;
- maximum row height;
- horizontal overflow;
- no-loss assignment count;
- grayscale clarity;
- edit-mode layout stability.

Acceptance targets:

- 212/212 assignments represented;
- no page-level horizontal overflow;
- no Marathi truncation;
- no unnecessary nested card borders;
- no orphan section headings;
- no large avoidable blank gaps;
- no unreadably small text;
- BIMA/रिक्त/बंद distinguishable without color alone;
- edit hover does not move content;
- print output remains content-driven.

---

# 8. Recommended implementation order

1. **Do not begin React yet.**
2. Refactor/create the Compact-v4 visual prototype around the existing `ChartDocument`.
3. Render the real 10/09 data into it.
4. Compare it against the two screenshots and the original PDF.
5. Iterate on geometry and density.
6. Only after the visual direction is accepted:
   - build the React screen;
   - port the grouped Kushal navigation;
   - connect the existing assignment CRUD;
   - keep the server renderer for print/PDF.
7. Add WIP/Coming Soon navigation entries for the deferred product areas.

No code has been changed for this design discussion. The next implementation should begin with the **static Compact-v4 chart prototype**, not generic React scaffolding.

